\frontmatter
\tableofcontents*
\mainmatter

\chapter{Introduction}

The \pkg{cetzDevice} package provides a graphics output device for \lang{R}
that records plots in a Typst-friendly format. The device transforms plotting
commands issued by \lang{R} functions into Typst code blocks. When included
in a paper typeset by Typst, these blocks are interpreted with the help of
CeTZ, a drawing package written by Johannes Wolf, fenjalien, and contributors.
Typst handles the text in the plot as well as the text in the surrounding
document. Mathematical formulae and native Typst content can therefore be
used in labels and annotations.

The device works with base graphics and grid graphics. This includes plots
produced by \pkg{ggplot2}, \pkg{lattice}, and \pkg{patchwork}. Figures are
stored as editable source, with raster images embedded in the same file.

This document is divided into three parts. Part~\ref{part:usage} describes
the package functions and gives examples of their use. Part~\ref{part:installation}
describes the installation of Typst and CeTZ. Part~\ref{part:internals}
describes the implementation of the device, including its use of Typst to
calculate font metrics.

\section{Acknowledgements}

This package follows the example of \pkg{tikzDevice}, originally written by
Charlie Sharpsteen and Cameron Bracken and maintained by Ralf Stubner and
contributors. Their work showed how an \lang{R} graphics device could use a
document typesetter both to measure labels and to produce the final figure.
The organization of this manual and the README follows their documentation.
The page layout and heading styles are adapted from their manual style file.
The source project is available at
\url{https://github.com/daqana/tikzDevice}.

We would like to thank Johannes Wolf, fenjalien, and the CeTZ contributors
for the drawing library on which this device depends. CeTZ provides the
paths, content nodes, named anchors, and canvas transformations used in the
generated figures. Its source and manual are available at
\url{https://github.com/cetz-package/cetz}.

We also thank the \lang{R} Core Team and Paul Murrell for the \lang{R}
graphics engine and its documentation, the Typst developers for the typesetter,
and the MiTeX developers for the interpretation of \LaTeX{} labels.

\part{Usage and Examples}\label{part:usage}

\chapter{Loading the Package}

The package is loaded in the usual way:

<<loading,eval=FALSE>>=
library(cetzDevice)
@

The main function, \code{cetz()}, opens a graphics device. Plotting commands
then draw on that device until it is closed with \code{dev.off()}. The output
file is written when the device closes. \code{cetz_save()} performs these
steps for a plot object or a function containing drawing commands.

\section{Options That Affect Package Behavior}

The settings described here are arguments to \code{cetz()}. They apply to
one device and may also be passed through \code{cetz_save()}.

\subsection{The \pkgopt{metrics} and \pkgopt{typst} Arguments}

By default, the device runs \code{typst query} to obtain text measurements.
The \pkgopt{typst} argument gives the path to the executable; its default is
\code{Sys.which("typst")}. A working Typst installation is checked before
the device is opened.

The alternative \code{metrics = "systemfonts"} uses the \pkg{systemfonts}
package. This is faster for plots containing many different labels and does
not run Typst during export. Its measurements approximate Typst's text
layout. This backend accepts plain text labels.

\subsection{The \pkgopt{family} and \pkgopt{font_map} Arguments}

The default family is Arial. The generic families \code{sans}, \code{serif},
\code{mono}, and \code{symbol} map to Arial, Times New Roman, Courier New,
and Symbol. The mapping can be replaced:

<<font-map,eval=FALSE>>=
cetz("figure.typ", family = "Libertinus Serif",
     font_map = c(sans = "Libertinus Sans",
                  serif = "Libertinus Serif",
                  mono = "DejaVu Sans Mono", symbol = "Symbol"))
plot(pressure)
dev.off()
@

Use a family installed on the machine that exports the plot and on the
machine that compiles it. The \pkg{systemfonts} backend records the resolved
system family in the output, including a substitute for a missing family.

\subsection{The \pkgopt{cache_dir} and \pkgopt{cache_key} Arguments}

Measurements are shared by devices in the same \lang{R} session. Setting
\pkgopt{cache_dir} also saves them on disk for use in later sessions.
The cache distinguishes label content, font settings, backend versions,
preambles, and statically named local dependencies.

Changing \pkgopt{cache_key} starts a separate set of measurements. This is
useful after installing fonts or changing a dependency whose filename is
computed by Typst code. The cache functions are described in
Chapter~\ref{chap:metrics}.

\subsection{The \pkgopt{text_mode} Argument}

Unmarked labels are plain text by default. Setting \code{text_mode = "typst"}
interprets them as Typst markup; \code{text_mode = "latex"} interprets them
as \LaTeX{} text through MiTeX 0.2.6. Both modes require
\code{metrics = "typst"}.

The functions \code{cetz_typst()} and \code{cetz_latex()} mark individual
labels. They can be used when the rest of the plot contains ordinary text.
Mathematics uses dollar delimiters in both syntaxes.

\subsection{The \pkgopt{preamble} Argument}

The \pkgopt{preamble} is a character vector of Typst source lines inserted
into both the measurement document and the output. Relative imports are
resolved beside the output file. Definitions used by a Typst label must be
made available in the \code{r-label-scope} dictionary:

<<preamble-example>>=
cetz("preamble.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif", preamble = c(
       "#let term = [Shared definition]",
       "#let r-label-scope = (term: term)"))
plot(1:3, xlab = cetz_typst("#term"))
dev.off()
@

\subsection{The \pkgopt{lwd_unit} Argument}

The value of \pkgopt{lwd_unit} is the number of Typst points used for an
\lang{R} line width of one. The default is 0.75, corresponding to one
ninety-sixth of an inch. Typst uses 72 points per inch.

\subsection{The \pkgopt{onefile} Argument}

With \code{onefile = TRUE}, every page is saved in a single module. A page
is selected in Typst with \code{r-figure(page: 2)}. The exported
\code{r-figures} array contains the individual page functions.

With \code{onefile = FALSE}, the filename must contain one integer
placeholder, for example \code{figure-\%03d.typ}. Each page is then written
to its own file. Console output requires \code{onefile = TRUE}.

\chapter{The \code{cetz} Function}\label{chap:cetz}

\section{Description}

The \code{cetz()} function opens an \lang{R} graphics device which supports
output in the CeTZ drawing language. The resulting Typst module can be
imported into a document or placed inside a CeTZ canvas.

The module exports \code{r-figure()}, \code{r-figures}, \code{r-draw()},
and \code{r-elements()}. The first two produce complete plots. The latter
two allow the plot to be combined with other CeTZ drawings.

\section{Usage}

<<cetz-usage,eval=FALSE>>=
cetz(file = "Rplots.typ", width = 7, height = 7,
     pointsize = 12, family = "Arial", bg = "white",
     standalone = FALSE, metrics = c("typst", "systemfonts"),
     typst = Sys.which("typst"),
     font_map = c(sans = "Arial", serif = "Times New Roman",
                  mono = "Courier New", symbol = "Symbol"),
     text_mode = c("plain", "typst", "latex"),
     preamble = character(), cache_dir = NULL,
     cache_key = "default", fg = "black", lwd_unit = 0.75,
     onefile = TRUE, console = FALSE)
@

The \pkgopt{file} argument gives the output path. The containing directory
must exist. Width and height are in inches; \pkgopt{pointsize} is the initial
text size in points. The \pkgopt{bg} and \pkgopt{fg} arguments give the
starting background and foreground colors. An alpha channel may be used,
including \code{bg = "transparent"}.

The \code{cetz_save()} function accepts the same device arguments:

<<save-usage,eval=FALSE>>=
cetz_save("figure.typ", function() plot(pressure),
          width = 6, height = 4)
@

It also accepts a plot object which can be drawn with \code{print()}, such
as a \pkg{ggplot2} object. The device is closed if drawing fails, and the
original error is reported to the caller. On success, \code{cetz_save()}
returns the output path invisibly; \code{cetz()} returns the device number
invisibly.

\section{Font Size Calculations}

The device reports its dimensions to \lang{R} in points, with 72 points to
the inch. A label's size is obtained from the graphical parameters supplied
by the graphics engine. In base graphics this includes the device point
size and \code{cex}; grid supplies its own font size and expansion factor.
The resulting size is used for both measurement and output.

Plot layout is calculated by \lang{R} during export. Axis labels, margins,
legends, and facets therefore depend on the selected family and size.
Choose the final figure dimensions and font before drawing the plot.
Changing text styles in an imported module does not run the \lang{R}
layout calculations again.

\subsection{UTF-8 Output}

The output is written as UTF-8. Ordinary labels are quoted as Typst strings,
so characters such as underscores and percent signs can be used directly.
Unicode characters require a font containing the corresponding glyphs.
Typst's \code{typst fonts} command lists the families it can find.

\section{Examples}

\subsection{Default Mode}

The default output is a module intended for inclusion in another document.
For example, the following commands produce a scatter plot with a fitted
regression line:

<<default-mode>>=
cetz("default.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif")
plot(mtcars$wt, mtcars$mpg, xlab = "Weight", ylab = "MPG")
abline(lm(mpg ~ wt, data = mtcars), col = "red", lwd = 2)
dev.off()
@

The file is included in a Typst document as follows:

\begin{typstcode}
#import "default.typ": r-figure

#figure(
  r-figure(),
  caption: [Vehicle weight and fuel economy.],
)
\end{typstcode}

\expandafter\def\csname captiondefault\endcsname{Output from the default mode.}
\manualfigure{default}{0.68}

\subsection{Raw Element Mode}

Every module exports \code{r-elements()} for drawing in an existing canvas.
This corresponds to the use of \code{bareBones} output in \pkg{tikzDevice}.
The coordinates are numeric Typst points, so the canvas length should be
set to \code{1pt}:

\begin{typstcode}
#import "default.typ": r-elements
#import "@preview/cetz:0.5.3" as cetz

#cetz.canvas(length: 1pt, {
  import cetz.draw: *
  r-elements()
})
\end{typstcode}

The default raw elements omit \lang{R}'s clipping containers. Passing
\code{clip: true} retains panel clipping, including text, images, and line
widths. This mode also applies canvas transformations to the whole clipped
layer. Unclipped elements follow CeTZ's native transformation rules for
content and physical stroke widths.

To place the complete plot at a named position, use \code{r-draw()}:

\begin{typstcode}
#import "default.typ": r-draw
#import "@preview/cetz:0.5.3" as cetz

#cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  r-draw(origin: (0, 0), angle: 0deg, name: "plot")
})
\end{typstcode}

This preserves the plot's physical size and clipping. Rotation, scaling,
shearing, and reflection of the surrounding canvas also transform its
text, strokes, images, gradients, and named anchors.

\subsection{Stand Alone Mode}

Setting \code{standalone = TRUE} adds page settings and draws all recorded
pages. The resulting file can be compiled directly:

<<standalone-mode>>=
cetz("standalone.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif", standalone = TRUE)
plot(pressure, type = "b")
dev.off()
@

\begin{shellcode}
typst compile standalone.typ standalone.svg
\end{shellcode}

For a multipage SVG export, use a page placeholder:

\begin{shellcode}
typst compile standalone.typ 'standalone-{p}.svg'
\end{shellcode}

A stand-alone file can still be imported as a module.

\subsection{Console Output Mode}

With \code{console = TRUE}, the module is written to standard output when
the device closes. An empty filename selects the same mode. The output
can be captured for further processing:

<<console-mode,eval=FALSE>>=
code <- capture.output({
  cetz(file = "", width = 4, height = 3)
  plot(pressure)
  dev.off()
})
@

\subsection{Using Typst and \LaTeX{} Labels}

The functions \code{cetz_typst()} and \code{cetz_latex()} mark labels for
typesetting in the requested syntax. Ordinary strings in the same plot
remain plain text.

<<math-labels>>=
cetz("math.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif")
plot(1:5, (1:5)^2,
     xlab = cetz_typst("$alpha^2$"),
     ylab = cetz_latex("$\\frac{x^2}{\\sigma}$"))
text(3, 20, cetz_typst("$integral_0^1 x^2 dif x$"))
dev.off()
@

\expandafter\def\csname captionmath\endcsname{Typst and MiTeX mathematics in the same plot.}
\manualfigure{math}{0.68}

For plots in which every label uses one syntax, set \pkgopt{text_mode} at
device creation. MiTeX implements a subset of \LaTeX{} commands; it does
not load \LaTeX{} packages or execute TikZ drawings. Existing formula
labels written for \pkg{tikzDevice} can be used where their commands are
supported by MiTeX.

\subsection{Annotating Graphics with CeTZ Commands}

The \code{cetz_annotate()} function inserts CeTZ code into the drawing.
\code{cetz_coord()} gives a name to an \lang{R} position, and
\code{cetz_node()} places a content node at that position. Base graphics
coordinates are interpreted in the current plot's user coordinate system.

<<annotations>>=
cetz("annotations.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif")
plot(1:5, (1:5)^2)
cetz_coord(4, 16, "marked")
cetz_node(2, 20, cetz_latex("$y=x^2$"), name = "label",
          options = 'frame: "rect", padding: 3')
cetz_annotate(
  'line("label.east", "marked", stroke: red, mark: (end: ">"))')
dev.off()
@

\expandafter\def\csname captionannotations\endcsname{A CeTZ node and arrow added to a base plot.}
\manualfigure{annotations}{0.68}

Raw CeTZ code uses device points measured from the lower left corner of
the page. Annotations are clipped to the page by default. Passing
\code{clip = TRUE} uses the current \lang{R} clipping rectangle instead.
Names remain available in later clipping layers, including anchors such
as \code{label.north}. A grouped plot exposes these as
\code{plot.label.north} when \code{r-draw(name: "plot")} is used.

The grid equivalents return grobs, which are placed when drawn:

<<grid-annotations>>=
cetz("grid.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif")
grid::grid.newpage()
grid::pushViewport(grid::viewport(xscale = c(0, 5),
                                  yscale = c(0, 25)))
grid::grid.draw(cetz_coord_grob(4, 16, "marked"))
grid::grid.draw(cetz_node_grob(2, 20, "A grid label", name = "label"))
grid::grid.draw(cetz_annotate_grob(
  'line("label.east", "marked", stroke: red)'))
grid::popViewport()
dev.off()
@

Numeric grid positions use \code{native} units by default. Explicit unit
objects can be used instead. Each node accepts one position and one text,
numeric, or logical label. Its text follows the viewport rotation and
graphical parameters, including color and alpha. Multiple nodes can be
combined with \code{grid::grobTree()}.

\subsection{Plotmath and Unicode Characters}

The \lang{R} graphics engine interprets \code{expression()} labels and sends
the resulting character and drawing operations to the device. These labels
retain \lang{R}'s plotmath layout. To obtain native Typst mathematics, use
\code{cetz_typst()} instead.

<<plotmath-example>>=
cetz("plotmath.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif")
plot(1:5, main = expression(alpha^2 + beta^2 == gamma^2),
     xlab = "Observation", ylab = "Value")
dev.off()
@

\subsection{Using ggplot2}

A ggplot object can be passed directly to \code{cetz_save()}. Set the theme
family to the one used in the document:

<<ggplot-example>>=
library(ggplot2)
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg, color = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_point() +
  labs(color = "Cylinders") +
  theme_minimal(base_family = "Libertinus Serif")
cetz_save("ggplot.typ", p, width = 4.5, height = 3,
          family = "Libertinus Serif")
@

\expandafter\def\csname captionggplot\endcsname{A ggplot2 scatter plot with a discrete legend.}
\manualfigure{ggplot}{0.68}

\subsection{Raster Images}

The device accepts \code{rasterImage()}, \code{grid.raster()},
\code{geom_raster()}, and raster color bars. Images are encoded as PNG
bytes in the module, so no separate image files need to accompany it.
Identical images share a resource even when drawn on different pages.

<<raster-example>>=
data <- expand.grid(x = 1:20, y = 1:15)
data$value <- with(data, sin(x / 3) * cos(y / 4))
p <- ggplot(data, aes(x, y, fill = value)) +
  geom_raster() + scale_fill_viridis_c() + coord_equal() +
  theme_minimal(base_family = "Libertinus Serif")
cetz_save("raster.typ", p, width = 4.5, height = 3,
          family = "Libertinus Serif")
@

\expandafter\def\csname captionraster\endcsname{Embedded raster data with vector axes, labels, and legend text.}
\manualfigure{raster}{0.68}

Image transparency, rotation, and reflected extents are retained. The
\code{interpolate} flag selects Typst's \code{smooth} or \code{pixelated}
image scaling.

\subsection{Gradient Fills}

Linear gradients and radial gradients with nested circles are supported
on rectangles, circles, polygons, and compound paths. Fills are embedded
as SVG vectors; CeTZ draws their outlines.

<<gradient-example>>=
cetz("gradient.typ", width = 4.5, height = 2.5,
     family = "Libertinus Serif")
grid::grid.newpage()
grid::grid.rect(width = 0.85, height = 0.75,
  gp = grid::gpar(col = NA, fill = grid::linearGradient(
    c("#2673b8", "#e6b450", "#d45d36"), group = FALSE)))
dev.off()
@

\expandafter\def\csname captiongradient\endcsname{A linear gradient embedded as vector content.}
\manualfigure{gradient}{0.68}

The gradient retains its device-space endpoints, color stops, alpha, and
\code{pad}, \code{repeat}, \code{reflect}, or \code{none} extension mode.
Radial circles must have different radii, with one circle contained in the
other. Reversed radii are handled by swapping the circles and color stops.
Other radial geometry produces an error.

With \lang{R} 4.2 or later, ggplot2 can request a vector gradient color bar:

<<gradient-guide,eval=FALSE>>=
p + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(display = "gradient"))
@

Tiling patterns, masks, nonrectangular clipping paths, glyph runs, and
advanced compositing are not implemented.

\subsection{Multiple Pages}

Successive plots are recorded as separate pages. By default, all pages
share one file and its image, gradient, and stroke resources. To write a
file for each page:

<<multiple-pages>>=
cetz("page-%03d.typ", width = 4.5, height = 3,
     family = "Libertinus Serif", onefile = FALSE,
     standalone = TRUE)
plot(pressure)
hist(iris$Sepal.Length, main = "Sepal length", xlab = "Length")
dev.off()
@

\chapter{The Text Measurement Cache}\label{chap:metrics}

\section{Description}

The device measures labels through the selected backend and keeps the
results in a cache. Repeated labels in the same font do not require
another Typst query. Cache entries contain the width, ascent, and descent
in device points.

The functions \code{cetz_cache_info()} and \code{cetz_clear_cache()}
inspect and clear this cache. Measurements can be requested through the
usual \lang{R} functions, including \code{strwidth()} and \code{strheight()},
while the device is active.

\section{Usage}

<<cache-usage,eval=FALSE>>=
cetz_cache_info()
cetz_clear_cache(cache_dir = NULL)
@

The first function returns a list containing \code{entries}, \code{hits},
\code{misses}, and \code{typst_calls}. The last count includes measurement
queries but not CLI version probes. The second function clears session
entries and resets these counts. If a directory is supplied, it also
removes the metric files belonging to this package from that directory.

\section{Examples}

<<cache-example,eval=FALSE>>=
cache <- tools::R_user_dir("cetzDevice", "cache")
cetz("figure.typ", family = "Libertinus Serif", cache_dir = cache)
plot.new()
strwidth("A measured label", units = "inches")
strwidth("A measured label", units = "inches")
dev.off()
cetz_cache_info()
@

The second request uses the cached result. Reopening a device with the
same settings reuses session entries. A new \lang{R} process can reuse
entries stored in \pkgopt{cache_dir}. Damaged disk entries are recomputed.

\part{Installation Guide}\label{part:installation}

\chapter{Obtaining a Typst Distribution}

The device requires \lang{R} 4.1.0 or later. The default measurement
backend and the exported figures require Typst 0.15.0 or later. Typst
provides a command-line executable for Windows, Linux, and macOS at
\url{https://github.com/typst/typst/releases}.

After installation, the following command should print the version:

\begin{shellcode}
typst --version
\end{shellcode}

The executable must be available to the \lang{R} session. Check its location
with \code{Sys.which("typst")}, or pass its full path to \code{cetz()}.

\section{Windows}

Download the Windows archive from the Typst releases page, extract it,
and add the directory containing \code{typst.exe} to \code{PATH}. Restart
\lang{R} after changing the environment. Building the \lang{R} package from
source also requires Rtools for the installed version of \lang{R}.

\section{UNIX/Linux}

Install Typst with the distribution's package manager or use the Linux
archive from the releases page. Check the installed version, since a
distribution may package an older release. A C compiler and the \lang{R}
development headers are needed to build \pkg{cetzDevice} from source.

\section{Mac OS X}

Typst can be installed with Homebrew:

\begin{shellcode}
brew install typst
\end{shellcode}

The release archives also contain macOS executables. Building this package
from source requires the Xcode Command Line Tools. An \lang{R} session
started from a graphical application may have a different \code{PATH}
from a terminal; the \pkgopt{typst} argument can specify the executable
directly.

\section{Installing CeTZ and Other Packages}

\subsection{Using the Typst Package Registry}

The generated module imports CeTZ 0.5.3 from the Typst preview registry.
Typst downloads it on first compilation and stores it in the package
cache. No separate CeTZ installation command is needed. \LaTeX{} labels
also import MiTeX 0.2.6.

The package version has four components. The first three identify the exact
CeTZ version; the fourth counts device releases for that version, starting
at one. For example, 0.5.3.2 is the second device release for CeTZ 0.5.3.
The device revision restarts at one when the CeTZ version changes.

Support starts with CeTZ 0.5.3; earlier CeTZ releases have not been adapted.
The function \code{cetz_backend()} reports the pinned CeTZ version and the
minimum Typst version.

\subsection{Manual Installation}

For an offline machine, populate Typst's package cache with CeTZ 0.5.3,
its dependencies, and MiTeX 0.2.6 if needed. Package directories and
environment variables are described at
\url{https://github.com/typst/packages}. Fonts used for export must also
be available on the machine that compiles the document.

Install the \lang{R} package from GitHub:

<<installation,eval=FALSE>>=
install.packages("remotes")
remotes::install_github("SchrodingerBlume/cetzDevice")
@

To install a local source checkout, run \code{R CMD INSTALL .} from its
root directory. The \pkg{systemfonts} package is optional and can be
installed with \code{install.packages("systemfonts")}.

\part{Package Internals}\label{part:internals}

\chapter{Introduction and Background}

An \lang{R} plotting function does not normally write a file itself. It
sends drawing operations to the graphics engine, which calls functions
provided by the active device. A device can draw on a screen, write an
image, or record the operations in another graphics language.

The \pkg{tikzDevice} package records those operations as TikZ source and
uses \LaTeX{} to measure text. The \pkg{cetzDevice} package takes the same
approach with CeTZ and Typst. The plotting packages do not need to know
which device is receiving their operations.

The C interface is implemented in \code{src/device.c}. It registers the
device callbacks and passes drawing operations to \lang{R}. Recording,
measurement, and serialization are implemented in the package's
\code{R} directory. The Typst scene code in \code{inst/typst/scene.typ}
handles shared drawing state and placement in a canvas.

\chapter{Anatomy of an R Graphics Device}

\section{Drawing Routines}

The device supplies callbacks for lines, polylines, polygons, rectangles,
circles, compound paths, text, and rasters. It also supplies gradient
callbacks. The graphics engine passes each callback the coordinates and
graphical context needed to draw the object.

The C functions convert their arguments to \lang{R} objects and call a
recording closure. The closure stores the operations in drawing order,
along with their text styles, stroke styles, and clipping state.
Repeated strokes, images, and gradients share definitions in the module.

Rectangular clipping is implemented with Typst content containers. The
complete drawing timeline is first resolved in a shared CeTZ context.
Names and anchors created in one layer therefore remain available to
later layers. The visible content of each layer is then clipped to its
recorded rectangle.

\section{Font Metric Routines}

The graphics engine asks the device for string widths and character
metrics while laying out a plot. The callbacks in \code{src/device.c}
delegate these requests to the backend in \code{R/metrics.R}.
Both measurement and output use the same font resolution and label
interpretation functions.

The device passes \lang{R}'s plotmath requests through its character
metric interface. Typst and MiTeX labels are measured as complete pieces
of content instead.

\section{Utility Routines}

The device reports its size and resolution to the engine and records
page changes. Closing the device finishes the final page, serializes the
module, and removes temporary measurement files. The output is assembled
before an existing file is replaced.

Drawing and measurement callbacks use unwind cleanup on \lang{R} 4.6
and later to release a device lock after a callback error.
\code{cetz_save()} lets the drawing call finish unwinding before closing
the device and reporting the error.

\chapter{Calculating Font Metrics}

Text measurements determine the positions of labels and the space used
by margins and legends. The Typst backend writes a small document using
the requested label, family, face, and point size. It calls
\code{typst query} and reads the numeric dimensions from a metadata
element. The same text expression is later written into the figure.

\section{Character Metrics}

The graphics engine requires a width, ascent, and descent for individual
characters. Typst measures a box using the text's bounds. Two zero-width
probe boxes, placed on opposite sides of the baseline, separate the
measured height into ascent and descent. The results are returned in
device points.

\section{Calling R Functions from C Functions}

The device stores references to an \lang{R} recording closure and an
\lang{R} measurement closure. These are preserved while the device is
open. Drawing callbacks build a call containing the operation type,
geometry, and graphical context. Measurement callbacks pass the label
and text style. The closures share the state of that particular device.

The \code{close} callback releases the preserved objects after writing
the output. Errors during final output are stored in the device state
and reported by \code{cetz_save()}.

\section{Implementing a System Call to Typst}

The measurement document is created beside the output file so that
relative preamble imports resolve in the same directory. The executable
is called through \code{system2()}, with paths quoted separately. The
query selects one metadata label and returns its JSON value.

Cache keys contain the backend version, platform, text expression,
preamble, relevant Typst environment variables, and local dependency
digests. Static local imports are followed recursively. Disk entries
are written through temporary files and are checked when read.

\chapter{On the Importance of Font and Style Consistency in Reports}

The font used in a plot affects its appearance and its layout. A label
measured in one family and drawn in another can extend past an axis or
overlap a neighboring label. The default backend asks Typst to measure
the same content that will appear in the figure.

Set the device family to the document's text family and use that family
in plotting themes. A document may use several font sizes, but a plot
should be exported at the size at which it will be read. Scaling the
whole plot also scales its text and strokes.

\section{Automatic Report Generation}

An analysis script can produce the figure modules before the report is
compiled. The Typst document then imports the modules just as it imports
other source files. A persistent measurement cache avoids repeating
queries for unchanged labels on later runs.

\begin{shellcode}
Rscript analysis.R
typst compile report.typ
\end{shellcode}

The output records graphics operations. Data mappings, statistical
transformations, and axis layout remain in the \lang{R} analysis, where
they can be rerun when the data or figure dimensions change.

